Gambatte (https://github.com/libretro/gambatte-libretro, GNU General Public License version 2) runs the Game Boy and
the Game Boy Color, and like every libretro core it says nothing of the instructions it runs. The tools apply this
change of Dreamports' own to its source before building it on the contributor's computer (tools/consoles.mjs,
tools/libretro/build.mjs), so the program that plays the original (tools/libretro/host.c) can ask to be told of each
one: its address, where that is in the cartridge's program whatever bank is switched in there, its first byte, and the
flags, A and the stack pointer. That's how prove learns which functions the original runs in each part of a game
(tools/lib/part-functions.mjs), and gameplay proven which instructions ran and which way each jump with a condition
went (tools/lib/played.mjs).

The processor only calls the function it was given, when it was given one, between reading an instruction and running
it: nothing of the console changes, so every frame's picture and sound are the same with it or without, which
tools/test/real-gb.mjs checks. Nothing of Gambatte is in the repository but the lines of its own this change sits
between.

diff --git a/libgambatte/libretro/libretro.cpp b/libgambatte/libretro/libretro.cpp
